% E-limitations.weft — The catalogue of what kcc does NOT (yet) do.
%
% A production compiler earns trust as much from an honest boundary as from
% its features.  This appendix is the single place that records every known
% limitation: what is unsupported, how kcc behaves when it meets that
% construct (refuse cleanly vs. not-yet-implemented), and why the boundary
% sits where it does.  The guiding rule throughout kcc is that an
% unsupported construct is REFUSED, never silently miscompiled.

\chapter{Known Limitations}
\label{ch:limitations}

\kcc{} compiles the large majority of real C17, but it is not complete.  This
appendix catalogues what it does \emph{not} do.  The list is deliberately
exhaustive and blunt: a compiler is only trustworthy if its boundary is
stated as plainly as its capabilities.  The invariant behind every entry is
that \kcc{} \emph{refuses} an unsupported construct with a diagnostic rather
than emitting wrong code---a clean error is a feature, silent miscompilation
never is.

\section{Language Features}

\paragraph{Variable-length arrays (VLAs).}
An array whose size is a run-time value, \texttt{int a[n]}, is not supported.
VLAs require the object's size to be computed at run time and the stack frame
to grow dynamically (a run-time \texttt{alloca}), plus run-time \texttt{sizeof}
and pointer arithmetic against a size that is not a compile-time constant.
\kcc{}'s frame layout is fixed at compile time, so a VLA declaration is
rejected.  Fixed-size and flexible-array-member forms are fully supported;
only the run-time-bound automatic array is not.

\paragraph{\texttt{\_Atomic} and \texttt{\_Complex}.}
The atomic type qualifier \texttt{\_Atomic} and the complex type specifiers
\texttt{\_Complex}/\texttt{\_Imaginary} (\stdref{6.7.2.4}, \stdref{6.2.5}) are
not implemented.  \texttt{\_Atomic} needs the lock-free atomic instruction
selection (load-acquire / store-release, \textsc{ldxr}/\textsc{stxr} loops) and
the \texttt{<stdatomic.h>} generic machinery; \texttt{\_Complex} needs a
two-component value representation and complex arithmetic lowering.  Both are
rejected in the declaration specifiers.

\paragraph{\texttt{\_\_int128} arithmetic.}\label{sec:lim-int128}
The GNU 128-bit integer type \texttt{\_\_int128} (and \texttt{unsigned
\_\_int128}) \emph{is} recognised as a type: it has size and alignment~16, lays
out correctly inside structs and unions, and---because kcc classifies a 16-byte
value like a two-eightbyte aggregate---can be copied, assigned with plain
\texttt{=}, passed and returned by value, and read or written through a union of
two \texttt{unsigned long} halves.  That is exactly the surface the Linux
kernel's \texttt{cmpxchg16b} path uses (\texttt{union \_\_u128\_halves} plus
inline assembly on the halves).

The self-hosting (Linux/x86-64) port additionally lowers the two 128-bit
operations the kernel's \texttt{math64.h} needs---an unsigned multiply and a
logical right shift, i.e.\ \texttt{mul\_u64\_u32\_shr} and
\texttt{mul\_u64\_u64\_shr}---by materialising a \texttt{\_\_int128} rvalue as a
sixteen-byte stack slot and computing on the two halves with ordinary 64-bit
operations (Section~\ref{sec:ir-int128}).  Everything else---add, subtract,
divide, modulo, left shift, comparison, compound assignment, unary
negation, and a \emph{signed} right shift---still needs carry-aware two-word
arithmetic that is not yet emitted, so the semantic analyser rejects it rather
than silently drop the high 64~bits.  The assembly reference goes further and
rejects \emph{all} \texttt{\_\_int128} arithmetic, as it does the other
kernel-only builtins: register-pair lowering with carry
(\textsc{adds}/\textsc{adc}) in the direct back ends remains future work.

\paragraph{Extended inline assembly beyond two operands.}
Basic inline assembly and the common two-operand extended forms
(\texttt{"=r"}, \texttt{"r"}, \texttt{"+r"}, \texttt{"i"}, with
\texttt{"memory"}/\texttt{"cc"} clobbers) work through the textual back end
(\S\ref{sec:um-inline-asm}).  Three or more live operands, memory
(\texttt{"m"}) or specific-register constraints, and \texttt{asm goto} are
refused with a diagnostic.  The obstacle is that an asm operand's virtual
register is carried on the AST node rather than in an instruction's operand
fields, so it is invisible to the middle-end passes (copy propagation,
common-subexpression elimination, dead-code elimination) and to the register
allocator.  Making \texttt{IR\_ASM} a first-class multi-operand instruction ---
operand vregs stored in an array that every pass iterates, with real
use/def/clobber sets --- is the deliberate future project that lifts this
ceiling (and also fixes the IR dumper, which currently faults on
\texttt{IR\_ASM}).

\paragraph{Attribute layout at member granularity.}
\texttt{\_\_attribute\_\_((packed))} and \texttt{aligned(N)} are honoured at the
aggregate level, but not on an individual member (\texttt{int x
\_\_attribute\_\_((packed));}).  Other attributes are parsed and ignored, which
is correct for the hint attributes but means member-level layout control is
absent.

\paragraph{Computed \texttt{goto}.}
The GNU label-address (\texttt{\&\&label}) and computed-goto (\texttt{goto
*p}) extension, used by some interpreter dispatch loops, is not supported.

\section{Standard Library and Headers}

\kcc{} ships a set of freestanding built-in headers (\texttt{stddef.h},
\texttt{stdint.h}, \texttt{stdarg.h}, \texttt{stdbool.h}, \texttt{limits.h},
\texttt{float.h}) and enough of \texttt{stdio.h}, \texttt{stdlib.h},
\texttt{string.h}, \texttt{math.h}, \texttt{fcntl.h}, and \texttt{unistd.h} to
build real programs against the system libraries.  It does \emph{not} provide a
full hosted standard library: many \texttt{<...>} headers are incomplete or
absent, and POSIX headers beyond the handful above are not modelled.  A program
that includes an unmodelled header, or uses a declaration \kcc{}'s headers do
not declare, will not compile until the missing declarations are supplied
(via \texttt{-I} and the real system headers, or by extending \kcc{}'s built-in
set).

\section{Code Generation and Targets}

\paragraph{Single target.}
\kcc{} targets ARM64 / Apple-silicon macOS (Mach-O) only.  The textual
\texttt{.s}-emission back end (\S\ref{ch:emit-asm}) is the groundwork for a
second target, but no other architecture or object format is implemented yet.

\paragraph{Optimisation scope.}
The optimiser performs constant folding, dead-code elimination, common-subexpression
elimination, a scalar mem2reg promotion, strength reduction, and copy
propagation.  It does \emph{not} perform loop optimisations, inlining,
vectorisation, or global (inter-procedural) analysis.  Generated code is
correct but not aggressively optimised.

\paragraph{Debug information granularity.}
Under \texttt{-g}, DWARF is emitted at function granularity (each function's
start), which is enough to name functions in a backtrace but not to map
individual statements or to describe local variables.

\section{The Standing Rule}

Every item above shares one property: when \kcc{} cannot compile a construct,
it says so.  It does not guess, it does not emit a plausible-looking wrong
instruction, and it does not silently drop the construct.  The boundary moves
outward as features land; until then, an honest refusal keeps every program
\kcc{} \emph{does} accept trustworthy.
